  ('UAL-0057', 'E227', 'Calcium hydrogen sulphite', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / antioxidant', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Dried fruit • Wine and cider • Bottled drinks • Prepared potato products', 'Minutes to hours', 'Liver • Enzymes', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '0.7 mg SO2 equivalent/kg body weight/day (temporary group ADI used in EFSA assessment)', 'Sulphites can trigger sensitivity reactions in susceptible people, including asthma-like symptoms. EFSA has identified exposure concerns for some population groups and continues to review the evidence base.', '• All sulphites E220–E228 count together as SO2 equivalents • Look out for dried fruit, wine/cider, bottled drinks and prepared potato products • Alcohol plus sulphites may make reactions more noticeable in sensitive people', '• People with sulphite sensitivity • People with asthma • Children with frequent dried fruit/juice intake • People prone to allergy-type reactions', 'Refrigeration • Freezing • Fermentation control • Fresh preparation • Ascorbic acid in some uses', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels. Sulphites above labelling thresholds must be declared as an allergen-style ingredient.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another sulphite form. In the Unboxed Library, this belongs with the whole E220–E228 group rather than being treated as separate in real life.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4438', '2026-07-05', 'Sulphite group entry. EFSA 2016 temporary group ADI and 2025 exposure update noted. Keep language focused on susceptible groups and cumulative SO2-equivalent exposure.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0058', 'E228', 'Potassium hydrogen sulphite', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / antioxidant', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Dried fruit • Wine and cider • Bottled drinks • Prepared potato products', 'Minutes to hours', 'Liver • Enzymes', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '0.7 mg SO2 equivalent/kg body weight/day (temporary group ADI used in EFSA assessment)', 'Sulphites can trigger sensitivity reactions in susceptible people, including asthma-like symptoms. EFSA has identified exposure concerns for some population groups and continues to review the evidence base.', '• All sulphites E220–E228 count together as SO2 equivalents • Look out for dried fruit, wine/cider, bottled drinks and prepared potato products • Alcohol plus sulphites may make reactions more noticeable in sensitive people', '• People with sulphite sensitivity • People with asthma • Children with frequent dried fruit/juice intake • People prone to allergy-type reactions', 'Refrigeration • Freezing • Fermentation control • Fresh preparation • Ascorbic acid in some uses', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels. Sulphites above labelling thresholds must be declared as an allergen-style ingredient.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is part of the same sulphite family. The key is recognising the group, not memorising every name.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4438', '2026-07-05', 'Sulphite group entry. EFSA 2016 temporary group ADI and 2025 exposure update noted. Keep language focused on susceptible groups and cumulative SO2-equivalent exposure.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0059', 'E234', 'Nisin', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Cheese • Dairy desserts • Heat-treated foods where permitted • Specialist preserved foods', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Gut', 'Varies / broken down like protein fragments*', '0.13 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Nisin is an antimicrobial peptide used to inhibit certain bacteria. EFSA noted exposure estimates were below the established ADI in the evaluated scenarios.', '• Mostly relevant in preserved dairy and heat-treated foods • Check alongside salt and other shelf-life techniques • Not the same issue profile as colours or sulphites', '• People with very specific preservative sensitivity • People tracking ultra-processed shelf-life additives', 'Fermentation control • Refrigeration • Pasteurisation • Shorter shelf-life recipes', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Nisin is a preservative used for microbial control. It is lower concern for most people, but still useful as a marker of a product designed for shelf life.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2006.314', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA nisin opinion notes SCF ADI 0.13 mg/kg bw and exposure below ADI in conservative assessment.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0060', 'E235', 'Natamycin', 'Preservatives', 'Surface preservative / antifungal', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Cheese rinds • Dry cured sausage surfaces • Surface-treated foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', 'Varies', '0–0.3 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Natamycin is an antifungal preservative used mainly on food surfaces. JECFA reaffirmed an ADI of 0–0.3 mg/kg body weight/day.', '• Mainly a surface treatment, not usually throughout the whole food • Check cheese rinds and cured sausage labels • Avoid assuming every cheese contains it', '• People avoiding antifungal preservatives • People with specific sensitivity • People who eat treated rinds/surfaces frequently', 'Traditional rind development • Refrigeration • Shorter shelf-life products', 'Approved for specific surface treatment uses and limits.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Natamycin is mostly a surface-preservation issue. The practical check is whether the rind or coating is part of what you eat.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://apps.who.int/food-additives-contaminants-jecfa-database/Home/Chemical/3255', '2026-07-05', 'JECFA 2024/2025 specifications reaffirm ADI. EFSA source also included in general references.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0061', 'E239', 'Hexamethylene tetramine', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Provolone cheese • Specialist cheese products where permitted', 'Hours', 'Acid breakdown • Liver', 'Varies', '0.15 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as formaldehyde', 'Hexamethylene tetramine is permitted only for very limited use. EFSA notes it breaks down under acidic conditions to formaldehyde, and its permitted use is restricted.', '• Only relevant to specific cheese use • Breakdown product is formaldehyde, so avoid casual daily exposure assumptions • Check specialist/imported cheese labels', '• Children consuming treated cheese frequently • People wanting to minimise formaldehyde-releasing additives • People with preservative sensitivity', 'Traditional cheese preservation • Refrigeration • Shorter shelf-life cheese', 'Approved for specific limited use only; use and residual levels are tightly restricted.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is a rare but important one. It is not an everyday additive for most people, but the chemistry makes it worth clear labelling and awareness.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/bg/efsajournal/pub/3696', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA E239 opinion: permitted only in Provolone cheese in EU context and assessed as formaldehyde residual/breakdown product.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0062', 'E242', 'Dimethyl dicarbonate', 'Preservatives', 'Beverage preservative / cold sterilant', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Soft drinks • Wine and alcohol-free wine • Liquid tea drinks • Flavoured drinks where permitted', 'Minutes to hours', 'Hydrolyses in drink • Liver handles breakdown products', 'Breaks down rapidly; residues not expected when used correctly*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated; permitted maximum use levels apply', 'Dimethyl dicarbonate is used to cold-sterilise certain drinks and rapidly hydrolyses in the beverage. EFSA assessed its use under permitted conditions.', '• Look for preserved drinks rather than solid foods • Breaks down to methanol and carbon dioxide, so formulation and permitted limits matter • Alcohol/soft drink context affects total exposure from the product', '• People checking preserved drinks • People avoiding chemical cold-sterilants • Children with frequent flavoured drink intake', 'Pasteurisation • Refrigeration • Shorter shelf-life drinks • Hot-fill processing', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is not a typical “stays in the food” preservative. It is used during drink processing and breaks down, so the important question is correct use and total drink pattern.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2015.4319', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2015 DMDC opinion: authorised for treatment of various beverages up to specified levels; hydrolyses rapidly in solution.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0063', 'E243', 'Ethyl lauroyl arginate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / antimicrobial', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Heat-treated meat products • Specialist preserved meats • Ready-to-eat meat products where permitted', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '0.5 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Used as an antimicrobial preservative. EFSA set an ADI of 0.5 mg/kg body weight/day and assessed authorised uses as safe within permitted limits.', '• Mainly relevant in processed meat • Check alongside nitrites, salt and other preservation systems • Product category limits matter', '• People eating processed meats frequently • People avoiding antimicrobial preservatives • Children with high processed meat intake', 'Refrigeration • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life recipes • Fermentation control', 'Approved for specific permitted uses and limits.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'A preservative to watch mainly in processed meat. The bigger Unboxed question is the full preservation stack, not just one additive.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0064', 'E249', 'Potassium nitrite', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / curing agent', '🔴 Highest Awareness', '5/5', '• Cured meats • Bacon-style products • Ham • Sausages • Processed meat products', 'Hours', 'Liver • Blood • Gut', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '0.07 mg/kg body weight/day as nitrite ion', 'Nitrites help control dangerous bacteria in cured meats, including Clostridium botulinum. EFSA considers permitted additive exposure generally within safe levels, but nitrosamine formation remains an important risk-management issue.', '• High-temperature cooking can increase nitrosamine concern • Processed meat frequency matters • Vitamin C/ascorbate may reduce nitrosamine formation in some contexts', '• Children with frequent processed meat intake • Pregnant people seeking clinical food guidance • People reducing processed meat exposure • People with cardiovascular or bowel health concerns', 'Fresh meat • Freezing • Refrigeration • Fermentation • Nitrite-free curing methods where genuinely used', 'Approved with strict permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Nitrites are not just another E number. They sit at the centre of the processed meat conversation, so frequency and cooking method matter.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0065', 'E250', 'Sodium nitrite', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / curing agent', '🔴 Highest Awareness', '5/5', '• Bacon • Ham • Sausages • Cured meats • Processed meat slices', 'Hours', 'Liver • Blood • Gut', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '0.07 mg/kg body weight/day as nitrite ion', 'Nitrites help protect against bacterial growth in cured meats. EFSA considers permitted uses protective, while nitrosamine formation remains an important concern in the wider processed meat context.', '• High-heat cooking of processed meat • Repeated processed meat intake • Often used with salt, smoke flavour and other preservatives', '• Children with frequent processed meat intake • People reducing processed meat • People with bowel health concerns • People tracking sodium/preservative load', 'Fresh meat • Freezing • Refrigeration • Traditional short shelf-life preparation', 'Approved with strict permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is one of the big ones to understand. It is useful for preservation, but it also explains why processed meat is different from fresh meat.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0066', 'E251', 'Sodium nitrate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / curing agent', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Cured meats • Long-cured meat products • Some cheeses where permitted • Traditional preserved products', 'Hours to days', 'Liver • Gut • Conversion to nitrite', '24–48 hrs+ varies*', '3.7 mg/kg body weight/day as nitrate ion', 'Nitrates can convert to nitrites in the body and in foods. EFSA set an ADI and notes total nitrate exposure from all dietary sources can be relevant.', '• Can convert to nitrite • Processed meat frequency matters • Vegetable nitrates are a different dietary context from cured meats', '• Children with frequent cured meat intake • People tracking processed meat exposure • People with nitrate-restricted medical advice', 'Fresh meat • Refrigeration • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life products', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Nitrate is not automatically the same story in every food. Context matters: cured meat is not the same as vegetables.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0067', 'E252', 'Potassium nitrate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / curing agent', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Cured meats • Dry-cured products • Traditional preserved meats • Some cheese uses where permitted', 'Hours to days', 'Liver • Gut • Conversion to nitrite', '24–48 hrs+ varies*', '3.7 mg/kg body weight/day as nitrate ion', 'Nitrates are used in curing and can convert to nitrites. EFSA concluded permitted additive uses are generally within safe levels, while total dietary nitrate can exceed the ADI in some scenarios.', '• Can convert to nitrite • Long-cured processed meats are the main label check • Total nitrate from the whole diet matters', '• Children with frequent cured meat intake • People reducing processed meat • People following nitrate-restricted advice', 'Fresh meat • Refrigeration • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life products', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This belongs in the processed meat awareness group. It is not about panic, it is about knowing what curing adds to the food.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0068', 'E280', 'Propionic acid', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / mould inhibitor', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Bakery products • Cakes • Cheese products • Packaged baked goods', 'Hours', 'Liver • Gut', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not limited', 'Mould inhibitor used mainly in bakery and cheese products. EFSA found no safety concern at authorised uses and levels.', '• Often signals longer shelf-life bakery products • Check with emulsifiers and flour treatment agents • Repeated packaged bread intake may increase exposure', '• People sensitive to preserved bakery products • Children eating packaged bread daily • People tracking ultra-processed bread', 'Shorter shelf-life bread • Sourdough fermentation • Freezing bread • Fresh bakery products', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Propionates are a classic bread shelf-life marker. The question is less ''is it poison?'' and more ''how processed is the everyday bread?''', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0069', 'E281', 'Sodium propionate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / mould inhibitor', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Wraps • Cakes • Bakery products • Cheese products', 'Hours', 'Liver • Gut', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not limited', 'Used to inhibit mould growth. EFSA re-evaluation reported no safety concern at authorised uses and levels.', '• Often appears in packaged bread/wraps • Look out for emulsifiers, gums and flour improvers in the same product • Daily bread habits matter', '• Children eating packaged bakery products daily • People sensitive to preserved bread • People tracking ultra-processed foods', 'Fresh bread • Sourdough • Freezing • Shorter ingredient lists', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is one of those additives that shows how modern bread is built for shelf life. Look at the whole label.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0070', 'E282', 'Calcium propionate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / mould inhibitor', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Rolls • Wraps • Cakes • Packaged bakery foods', 'Hours', 'Liver • Gut', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not limited', 'Calcium propionate is widely used to inhibit mould in bakery products. EFSA found no safety concern at authorised levels.', '• Common in packaged bread • Check with E471, E472e and other bread improvers • Daily exposure can come from normal bread habits', '• Children eating packaged bread daily • People sensitive to bread additives • People tracking mood/gut changes around bakery foods', 'Fresh bread • Sourdough • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life bakery products', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Calcium propionate is one to recognise because bread is eaten so often. It is the daily repeat that matters.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0071', 'E283', 'Potassium propionate', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative / mould inhibitor', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Bread products • Cakes • Cheese products where permitted', 'Hours', 'Liver • Gut', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not limited', 'Part of the propionate preservative group used to reduce mould growth. EFSA found no safety concern at authorised levels.', '• Belongs with E280–E282 exposure • Check long-life bakery products • Watch combined shelf-life additives', '• People sensitive to preserved bakery foods • Children with daily packaged bakery intake • People reducing ultra-processed bread', 'Fresh bakery products • Freezing • Sourdough • Shorter shelf-life recipes', 'Approved with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is part of the same bread-preservative family. Treat the group as the signal, not just the single E number.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0072', 'E284', 'Boric acid', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Caviar/sturgeon eggs only where permitted • Specialist fish roe products', 'Hours', 'Kidneys • Urine', 'Usually 24 hrs or less*', '0.16 mg boron/kg body weight/day', 'Boric acid has very limited food use. EFSA set a group ADI expressed as boron and noted reproductive toxicity findings at higher experimental exposures.', '• Only permitted in very limited specialist use • Boron exposure from all sources matters • Not an everyday UK food additive for most people', '• Children eating fish roe/caviar • Pregnant people seeking clinical food guidance • People with kidney issues • People avoiding boron preservatives', 'Salt curing • Refrigeration • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life roe products', 'Approved only for very limited permitted use in specific foods and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is rare, but it deserves awareness because the permitted use is narrow and the safety discussion is specific.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0073', 'E285', 'Sodium tetraborate; borax', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Caviar/sturgeon eggs only where permitted • Specialist fish roe products', 'Hours', 'Kidneys • Urine', 'Usually 24 hrs or less*', '0.16 mg boron/kg body weight/day', 'Sodium tetraborate belongs with boric acid in the boron group ADI. EFSA noted limited permitted use and toxicological concerns at higher exposures.', '• Only relevant to specific fish roe/caviar uses • Counts toward boron exposure • Not a general everyday preservative', '• Children eating fish roe/caviar • Pregnant people seeking clinical food guidance • People with kidney issues • People avoiding boron preservatives', 'Salt curing • Refrigeration • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life roe products', 'Approved only for very limited permitted use in specific foods and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is not a normal supermarket additive for most families, but it should be clearly explained when it appears.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0074', 'E1105', 'Lysozyme', 'Preservatives', 'Preservative enzyme / antimicrobial', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Cheese • Hard and semi-hard cheeses • Brewing processes in some contexts • Wine and vinegar production in some contexts', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Gut', 'Varies / digested like protein*', 'Not specified / not allocated', 'Lysozyme is usually derived from hen egg white and can act against certain bacteria. EFSA notes egg-derived lysozyme is a known food allergen.', '• Main issue is egg allergy • Check cheese labels if egg allergic • Not the same concern profile as synthetic preservatives', '• People with egg allergy • Parents of egg-allergic children • People avoiding enzyme preservatives', 'Starter culture control • Refrigeration • Traditional maturation controls', 'Approved with permitted uses and allergen relevance where derived from egg.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'The key with lysozyme is not fear. It is allergen awareness, especially for people who would not expect egg-related material in cheese.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0075', 'E300', 'Ascorbic acid', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / flour treatment aid', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Bread • Meat products • Soft drinks • Fruit products • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Ascorbic acid is vitamin C and is widely used as an antioxidant. EFSA/JECFA considered its use acceptable at food additive levels.', '• Can reduce oxidation • In nitrite-cured foods it may help reduce nitrosamine formation • In acidic drinks with benzoates, formulation control matters', '• People taking high-dose vitamin C supplements • People with specific medical advice around vitamin C intake', 'Citrus juice • Acerola • Fresh fruit ingredients • Rosemary extract in some uses', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is another reminder that an E number can also be a vitamin. Context and dose matter.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0076', 'E301', 'Sodium ascorbate', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / curing support', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Cured meats • Meat products • Bread • Processed foods • Drinks', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Sodium ascorbate is a vitamin C salt used as an antioxidant and sometimes alongside nitrites to help control nitrosamine formation.', '• Often paired with nitrites in cured meat • Adds sodium, usually small amounts • Consider supplement intake separately from food additive use', '• People tracking sodium intake • People taking high-dose vitamin C supplements • People reviewing processed meat labels', 'Ascorbic acid • Citrus extracts • Fresh preparation • Shorter shelf-life recipes', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Sodium ascorbate can be part of the preservation system. It may be there to reduce oxidation, not just to ''add chemicals''.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0077', 'E302', 'Calcium ascorbate', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Processed fruits • Meat products • Fortified foods • Drinks • Bakery products', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Calcium ascorbate is a vitamin C salt used as an antioxidant. EFSA/JECFA considered ascorbates acceptable at additive levels.', '• Contributes to vitamin C exposure • May appear with other antioxidants • Supplement intake is a separate consideration', '• People taking high-dose vitamin C supplements • People with specific medical advice on calcium/vitamin C intake', 'Ascorbic acid • Citrus juice • Fresh fruit ingredients', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is a lower-concern additive for most people. It is mainly about understanding function, not panic.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0078', 'E304', 'Fatty acid esters of ascorbic acid', 'Antioxidants', 'Fat-soluble antioxidant', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Fats and oils • Meat products • Bakery fats • Snacks • Foods containing added oils', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', '24–48 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Fatty acid esters of ascorbic acid are used to protect fats and oils from oxidation. They are related to vitamin C chemistry but used in fat-containing foods.', '• Look at the oil/fat quality in the product • Often part of a processed fat system • Check alongside emulsifiers and refined oils', '• People tracking ultra-processed fats • People sensitive to fatty processed foods • People taking high-dose supplements', 'Ascorbic acid • Mixed tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Better oil storage', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This one is less about the additive alone and more about the type of food it appears in — usually processed fats and oils.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0079', 'E306', 'Tocopherols', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / vitamin E source', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Vegetable oils • Cereals • Snacks • Fat-containing foods • Supplements', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Several days possible due to fat-soluble nature*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Tocopherols are vitamin E-related antioxidants used to protect fats and oils from oxidation. EFSA re-evaluated E306–E309 as food additives.', '• Fat-soluble, so supplement intake matters more than normal food additive exposure • Often used in oils and fatty foods • Check total processed fat quality', '• People taking high-dose vitamin E supplements • People on blood-thinning medication should seek medical advice before high-dose supplements', 'Natural vitamin E-rich oils • Rosemary extract • Better packaging/storage', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a low-concern additive for most people. The bigger question is the quality of the fats it is protecting.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0080', 'E307', 'Alpha-tocopherol', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / vitamin E form', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Oils • Cereals • Fat spreads • Snacks • Fortified foods', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Several days possible due to fat-soluble nature*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Alpha-tocopherol is a vitamin E form used as an antioxidant to protect fats and oils from oxidation.', '• Consider high-dose vitamin E supplements separately • Often appears in fat-containing processed foods • Oil quality still matters', '• People taking high-dose vitamin E supplements • People on blood-thinning medication should seek clinical advice before high-dose supplements', 'Mixed tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Better oil storage', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Alpha-tocopherol is not one of the big red-flag additives. It usually tells you the product contains fats that need protecting from oxidation.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0081', 'E308', 'Gamma-tocopherol', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / vitamin E form', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Oils • Snacks • Fat-containing foods • Cereals • Fortified products', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Several days possible due to fat-soluble nature*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Gamma-tocopherol is a vitamin E-related antioxidant used to protect fats and oils. EFSA assessed it within the tocopherol group.', '• Often found in fatty processed foods • Consider supplement intake separately • Not a colour/preservative-type concern', '• People taking high-dose vitamin E supplements • People reviewing processed fat intake', 'Mixed tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Better packaging/storage', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another vitamin E-related antioxidant. The additive is usually lower concern than the processed food pattern it may appear in.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0082', 'E309', 'Delta-tocopherol', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / vitamin E form', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Oils • Snacks • Fat-containing foods • Cereals • Fortified products', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Several days possible due to fat-soluble nature*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Delta-tocopherol is a vitamin E-related antioxidant used to protect fats and oils from oxidation. EFSA assessed it within the E306–E309 group.', '• Often appears where fats/oils need shelf-life support • Check overall fat quality • Supplement intake is separate from food additive exposure', '• People taking high-dose vitamin E supplements • People tracking processed fats', 'Mixed tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Better oil storage', 'Approved with permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a lower-awareness additive. It is more useful as a signpost to look at the fats and oils in the product.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/safety-hygiene/food-additives', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-05', 'Awaiting detailed EFSA/FSA review.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0083', 'E310', 'Propyl gallate', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Fats and oils • Chewing gum • Soup mixes • Seasonings • Processed potato products', 'Hours', 'Liver • Gut', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '0.5 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Propyl gallate is used to slow oxidation and rancidity in fat-containing foods. EFSA re-evaluated it and retained a conservative ADI for food additive use.', '• Check with other synthetic antioxidants such as E319, E320 and E321 • Most relevant in fatty processed foods and snack products • Repeated intake comes from the whole fat-preservation system', '• People eating fatty processed snacks frequently • People avoiding synthetic antioxidants • Children with high snack-food intake', 'Tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Ascorbyl palmitate • Better oil storage', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Propyl gallate is mainly a fat-protection additive. The Unboxed question is what kind of oils and processed fats it is protecting.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2014.3642', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2014 re-evaluation; ADI retained conservatively. Treat as synthetic antioxidant awareness, especially in fatty processed foods.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0084', 'E315', 'Erythorbic acid', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / curing support', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Cured meats • Preserved fish products • Processed meat • Some preserved foods', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '6 mg/kg body weight/day (SCF); JECFA ADI not specified', 'Erythorbic acid is an antioxidant related to vitamin C chemistry. It is often used in cured products to protect colour and help control nitrosamine formation.', '• Often appears with nitrites in cured meat • Look at the full processed meat system, not the antioxidant alone • Helps manage oxidation and curing reactions', '• People tracking processed meat intake • People avoiding cured meat additives • Children eating processed meat frequently', 'Ascorbic acid • Fresh preparation • Shorter shelf-life foods', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is usually there to manage oxidation in preserved foods. In cured meat, it can be part of the system used to control unwanted reactions.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4360', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2016 re-evaluation. SCF ADI 6 mg/kg bw/day noted; JECFA ADI not specified. Useful context with nitrites.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0085', 'E316', 'Sodium erythorbate', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / curing support', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Cured meats • Ham • Sausages • Preserved fish products • Processed meat products', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '6 mg/kg body weight/day as erythorbic acid (SCF); JECFA ADI not specified', 'Sodium erythorbate is used as an antioxidant and curing support ingredient. EFSA reviewed E315 and E316 together and did not identify concern at authorised uses.', '• Commonly used with nitrites in cured meat • Check the whole cured meat label: nitrite, salt, smoke flavour and stabilisers • Helps reduce oxidation and support cured colour', '• People tracking processed meat intake • Children eating cured meats frequently • People reducing preservative stacks', 'Ascorbic acid • Fresh meat • Freezing • Shorter shelf-life preparation', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Sodium erythorbate is not the same concern as nitrite itself, but it often appears in the same processed meat environment.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4360', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2016 re-evaluation with E315. Mostly relevant as part of curing/preservation systems.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0086', 'E319', 'Tertiary-butyl hydroquinone (TBHQ)', 'Antioxidants', 'Synthetic antioxidant', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Frying oils • Instant noodles • Crisps and snacks • Crackers • Fat-containing processed foods', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Usually 24–48 hrs*', '0.7 mg/kg body weight/day', 'TBHQ protects fats and oils from rancidity. EFSA/JECFA set an ADI and refined exposure assessments have focused on high consumers and fat-containing products.', '• Often used in oil/fat systems • Watch alongside E320 and E321 in snack foods • Repeated exposure can come from fried snacks, noodles and processed fats', '• Children eating many processed snacks • People eating instant noodles/fried snacks frequently • People avoiding synthetic antioxidants', 'Tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Ascorbyl palmitate • Better oil storage', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'TBHQ is one of the additives that points straight back to processed oils. Look at how often those foods are showing up in the day.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2016.4363', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2016 refined exposure assessment; ADI 0.7 mg/kg bw/day based on JECFA and EFSA confirmation.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0087', 'E320', 'Butylated hydroxyanisole (BHA)', 'Antioxidants', 'Synthetic antioxidant', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Chewing gum • Fats and oils • Snack foods • Cereal products • Processed potato products', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Usually 24–48 hrs*', '1 mg/kg body weight/day', 'BHA is used to prevent rancidity in fat-containing foods. EFSA exposure assessment found typical use did not exceed the ADI for most groups, but it remains a higher-awareness synthetic antioxidant.', '• Often paired with BHT or other antioxidants • Most relevant in fatty processed foods • Check total processed snack and oil exposure', '• Children eating many processed snacks • People avoiding synthetic antioxidants • People tracking highly processed fat intake', 'Tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Ascorbyl palmitate • Better oil storage', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'BHA is not about one bite of food. It is about how often processed oils, snacks and long shelf-life fats appear across the week.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2012.2759', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2012 exposure statement; ADI 1 mg/kg bw/day. Keep wording balanced; avoid overclaiming beyond authorised-use assessment.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0088', 'E321', 'Butylated hydroxytoluene (BHT)', 'Antioxidants', 'Synthetic antioxidant', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Chewing gum • Fats and oils • Snack foods • Cereals • Processed foods containing added fats', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Fat digestion', 'Usually 24–48 hrs*', '0.25 mg/kg body weight/day', 'BHT is a synthetic antioxidant used to protect fats from oxidation. EFSA established an ADI after re-evaluation, with exposure assessments focusing on high-consuming groups.', '• Often appears with other antioxidants in fat systems • Watch with E319 and E320 in snack/oil-based products • Total processed fat intake matters', '• Children eating processed snacks frequently • People avoiding synthetic antioxidants • People tracking fat-additive exposure', 'Tocopherols • Rosemary extract • Ascorbyl palmitate • Better oil storage', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'BHT is another signpost additive. When you see it, ask what fats need protecting and how often that food is being eaten.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2012.2588', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2012 re-evaluation; ADI 0.25 mg/kg bw/day. Higher awareness due to synthetic antioxidant use in processed fats.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0089', 'E392', 'Extracts of rosemary', 'Antioxidants', 'Natural antioxidant', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Oils and fats • Meat products • Snacks • Bakery fats • Fat-based spreads', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Liver • Gut • Fat digestion', '24–48 hrs*', 'Temporary ADI 0–0.3 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as carnosic acid plus carnosol', 'Rosemary extracts are used to slow oxidation in fats and oils. JECFA set a temporary ADI expressed as carnosic acid plus carnosol; EFSA refined exposure assessments for authorised uses.', '• Usually signals fat or oil preservation • Check the underlying oil quality and overall processed-fat pattern • Can replace synthetic antioxidants in some products', '• People with herb/rosemary sensitivity • People tracking processed fats • People using high-dose herbal extracts separately', 'Tocopherols • Ascorbyl palmitate • Better packaging and storage', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Rosemary extract is often a cleaner-label antioxidant, but it still tells you the food contains fats that need shelf-life protection.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2018.5373', '2026-07-05', 'JECFA temporary ADI 0–0.3 mg/kg bw/day as carnosic acid+carnosol; EFSA refined exposure assessment.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0090', 'E586', '4-Hexylresorcinol', 'Antioxidants', 'Antioxidant / colour retention in crustaceans', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Fresh crustaceans • Frozen crustaceans • Shrimps and prawns where permitted', 'Hours', 'Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not established; residue limits apply', '4-Hexylresorcinol is used to prevent melanosis, or black spotting, in crustaceans. EFSA/JECFA did not establish a numerical ADI but considered the specific use acceptable under residue limits.', '• Very specific use: crustaceans • Look for repeated seafood intake rather than general processed food exposure • Relevant as a residue-control additive, not a broad everyday additive', '• People eating treated prawns/shrimps frequently • People avoiding seafood processing aids/additives • People with seafood allergy should focus on seafood allergen risk separately', 'Rapid chilling • Freezing • Fresh preparation • Sulphite-free handling', 'Approved for specific permitted use and residue limits in crustaceans.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is a narrow-use additive. It matters most when people want to know what has been used to keep prawns and crustaceans looking fresh.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging/Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2014.3643', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2014 re-evaluation; no numerical ADI. Use linked to crustacean melanosis prevention and residue limits.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0091', 'E420 (i)', 'Sorbitol', 'Sweeteners', 'Sweetener / humectant / bulking agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Chewing gum • Protein bars • Diabetic/low-sugar foods • Some medicines and syrups', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Sorbitol is a polyol sweetener. It is lower calorie than sugar and less cariogenic, but large amounts can cause bloating, gas or laxative effects.', '• Polyol stacking with xylitol, maltitol, mannitol or erythritol • Over 10% added polyols requires a laxative warning • Effects depend strongly on dose and individual gut tolerance', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children consuming sugar-free sweets • People eating multiple polyol products in one day', 'Small amounts of sugar • Stevia • Monk fruit where permitted • Whole fruit sweetness', 'Approved. Products with more than 10% added polyols must carry a laxative-effect warning.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'The main issue with sorbitol is not toxicity; it is gut tolerance. The dose and the number of sugar-free products in the day matter.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-05', 'Polyol. ADI not specified; practical concern is laxative/gut effects at higher intakes and product labelling above 10% polyols.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0092', 'E420 (ii)', 'Sorbitol syrup', 'Sweeteners', 'Sweetener / humectant / bulking agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Chewing gum • Low-sugar desserts • Protein bars • Some medicines and syrups', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Sorbitol syrup is a polyol sweetener and humectant. Like sorbitol, higher intakes can cause digestive symptoms and laxative effects in sensitive people.', '• Counts with other polyols across the day • Watch sugar-free sweets, bars and chewing gum together • Dose matters more than the E number alone', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children consuming sugar-free sweets • People using several sugar-free products daily', 'Small amounts of sugar • Stevia • Whole fruit sweetness • Recipe reformulation', 'Approved. Products with more than 10% added polyols must carry a laxative-effect warning.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Sorbitol syrup belongs in the gut-awareness group. One product may be fine; several sugar-free products can be very different.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-05', 'Polyol syrup form. Same practical cautions as sorbitol: gastrointestinal tolerance and cumulative polyol intake.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0093', 'E421', 'Mannitol', 'Sweeteners', 'Sweetener / bulking agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Chewing gum • Low-sugar foods • Table-top sweetener blends • Some medicines', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Mannitol is a polyol sweetener. It contributes fewer calories than sugar and is less cariogenic, but higher intakes can cause bloating or laxative effects.', '• Polyol stacking with sorbitol, maltitol, xylitol or erythritol • Over 10% added polyols requires a laxative warning • Gut effects vary by person and dose', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People using several polyol products daily', 'Small amounts of sugar • Stevia • Whole fruit sweetness • Product reformulation', 'Approved. Products with more than 10% added polyols must carry a laxative-effect warning.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Mannitol is another reminder that sugar-free does not always mean consequence-free. For some people the gut feels it first.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://data.food.gov.uk/regulated-products/food_authorisations/e-421', '2026-07-05', 'Polyol. ADI not specified. Use NHS/FSA wording on laxative effects; avoid overstating systemic effects.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0094', 'E950', 'Acesulfame K', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Diet drinks • Sugar-free squash • Low-calorie yoghurts • Chewing gum • Protein drinks', 'Hours', 'Absorbed • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '15 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Acesulfame K is a high-intensity sweetener. EFSA re-evaluated it in 2025 and set a revised ADI of 15 mg/kg body weight/day, with exposure estimates generally below the ADI.', '• Often blended with aspartame, sucralose or other sweeteners • Look at repeated diet drinks across the day • Acidic drinks and flavour systems can increase total additive exposure', '• Children with high diet drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to very sweet products', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks • Fruit/herb flavouring', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Acesulfame K is less about one can and more about the habit: diet drink, protein drink, yoghurt, gum — all in the same day.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2025.9317', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2025 re-evaluation set revised ADI 15 mg/kg bw/day. Commonly blended with other high-intensity sweeteners.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0095', 'E951', 'Aspartame', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Diet drinks • Sugar-free squash • Chewing gum • Low-calorie desserts • Table-top sweeteners', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver • Amino acid metabolism', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '40 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Aspartame is broken down into components also found in normal foods. EFSA reaffirmed safety at the ADI, while people with PKU must avoid or strictly manage phenylalanine intake.', '• Often blended with acesulfame K • Repeated diet drinks can drive exposure • PKU warning is essential because of phenylalanine', '• People with PKU • Children with high diet drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to diet drinks', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks • Fruit/herb flavouring', 'Approved. Foods containing aspartame must indicate that it contains a source of phenylalanine.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Aspartame is one of the most talked-about sweeteners. The key public warning is PKU; for everyone else, the bigger Unboxed question is total diet-drink habit.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2013 full risk assessment; ADI 40 mg/kg bw/day. Include PKU warning consistently. 2023 IARC/JECFA context should be handled carefully if expanded.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0096', 'E952', 'Cyclamic acid and its Na and Ca salts', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Low-calorie drinks • Table-top sweeteners • Sugar-free desserts • Low-sugar confectionery', 'Hours', 'Gut bacteria • Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '7 mg/kg body weight/day (EU/EFSA); JECFA ADI 0–11 mg/kg bw', 'Cyclamates are high-intensity sweeteners. EU/EFSA authorisation uses a lower ADI than JECFA, and exposure can be more relevant for children or high consumers of sweetened drinks.', '• Often blended with saccharin or other sweeteners • Gut bacteria can convert some cyclamate to cyclohexylamine in some people • Repeated low-calorie drinks matter most', '• Children with high diet drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People with sensitive digestion • People avoiding cyclamate due to regulatory differences', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks', 'Approved in the UK/EU framework with permitted categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'Cyclamate is a good example of why regulation needs context. It is approved here, but the daily pattern and child exposure are worth watching.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://apps.who.int/food-additives-contaminants-jecfa-database/Home/Chemical/995', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA/EU ADI commonly 7 mg/kg bw/day; JECFA ADI 0–11 mg/kg bw. Note regulatory divergence without overstating.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0097', 'E953', 'Isomalt', 'Sweeteners', 'Sweetener / bulking agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Chewing gum • Low-sugar chocolate • Diabetic/low-sugar foods • Cake decorations', 'Hours', 'Gut fermentation • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Isomalt is a polyol used to replace sugar and add bulk. It is less cariogenic than sugar, but larger amounts can cause gas, bloating or laxative effects.', '• Polyol stacking with sorbitol, maltitol, xylitol or erythritol • Sugar-free sweets can deliver high polyol doses quickly • Effects are dose-related and gut-specific', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People using several polyol products daily', 'Small amounts of sugar • Stevia blends • Whole food sweetness • Recipe reformulation', 'Approved. Products with more than 10% added polyols must carry a laxative-effect warning.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Isomalt is one of the reasons sugar-free sweets can still hit the body. The label may say no sugar; your gut may say otherwise.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-05', 'Polyol. Main public-facing issue: laxative/gut effects from cumulative intake. ADI not specified.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0098', 'E954', 'Saccharin and its Na, K and Ca salts', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Table-top sweeteners • Diet drinks • Sugar-free foods • Chewing gum • Low-calorie desserts', 'Hours', 'Absorbed • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', '9 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Saccharin is a high-intensity sweetener. EFSA re-evaluated saccharins in 2024, concluded they are safe at authorised uses and increased the ADI to 9 mg/kg body weight/day.', '• Often blended with cyclamate or other sweeteners • Repeated use from drinks and tabletop sweeteners increases exposure • Bitter aftertaste may be masked with blends', '• Children with high diet drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to sweetener blends', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Saccharin has old controversy attached to it, but EFSA''s newer review raised the ADI. The practical focus is still how many sweetener sources stack up.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/plain-language-summary/re-evaluation-saccharin-and-its-sodium-potassium-and-calcium-salts-e-954', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2024 re-evaluation: ADI 9 mg/kg bw/day expressed as free imide; old rat bladder tumour concern no longer considered relevant to humans at authorised use.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0099', 'E955', 'Sucralose', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Diet drinks • Sugar-free squash • Protein powders • Low-calorie desserts • Table-top sweeteners', 'Hours', 'Mostly not metabolised • Gut • Faeces/urine', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '15 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Sucralose is a high-intensity sweetener. EFSA''s 2026 re-evaluation found authorised uses safe at current levels and retained the ADI, while not confirming safety for a proposed new extension of use.', '• Often blended with acesulfame K or other sweeteners • Repeated diet drinks and protein products can stack exposure • Heating/baking context should be handled carefully by product rules', '• Children with high diet drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to sweetened protein products', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks • Fruit/herb flavouring', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Sucralose is common because it is very sweet and stable. The Unboxed focus is daily sweetener pattern, not one isolated product.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/plain-language-summary/re-evaluation-sucralose-e-955-food-additive', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2026 re-evaluation retained ADI 15 mg/kg bw/day for authorised uses. Avoid broad microbiome claims unless expanded with specific review evidence.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0100', 'E957', 'Thaumatin', 'Sweeteners', 'Natural high-intensity sweetener / flavour modifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Table-top sweeteners • Chewing gum • Low-sugar foods • Flavoured products • Confectionery', 'Hours', 'Digested like protein', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Thaumatin is a sweet-tasting protein extracted from the katemfe fruit. EFSA re-evaluated it and did not identify safety concerns at authorised uses.', '• Often used in very small amounts because it is intensely sweet • May appear in sweetener blends • Allergen risk appears low but protein nature should be noted', '• People tracking sweetener blends • People with unusual plant-protein sensitivities • Children with frequent low-sugar product intake', 'Whole fruit sweetness • Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Thaumatin is one of the lower-concern sweeteners, but it still belongs in the pattern check when low-sugar products stack up.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8630604/', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2021 re-evaluation; natural plant protein sweetener. ADI not specified/not allocated.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0101', 'E959', 'Neohesperidine DC', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener / flavour modifier', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Low-calorie drinks • Chewing gum • Confectionery • Table-top sweeteners • Flavoured products', 'Hours', 'Gut bacteria • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '20 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Neohesperidine DC is a high-intensity sweetener derived from citrus flavanones. EFSA re-evaluated it in 2022 and derived an ADI of 20 mg/kg body weight/day, with exposure estimates below the ADI.', '• Often used in sweetener blends • Can also modify flavour and mask bitterness • Repeated low-calorie drinks and confectionery can stack exposure', '• Children with high low-calorie drink intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to sweetener blends', 'Steviol glycosides • Small amounts of sugar • Unsweetened drinks • Citrus flavouring without sweetener', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'This is less common in everyday conversation, but it is part of the sweetener-blend world. Look at the full label, not just the familiar names.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2022.7595', '2026-07-05', 'EFSA 2022 re-evaluation set ADI 20 mg/kg bw/day. Often used in blends/flavour modification.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0102', 'E960a', 'Steviol glycosides from stevia', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Low-calorie drinks • Table-top sweeteners • Protein products • Low-sugar yoghurts • Sugar-free snacks', 'Hours', 'Gut bacteria • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '4 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as steviol equivalents', 'Steviol glycosides are sweeteners derived from stevia leaves. EFSA/JECFA established an ADI expressed as steviol equivalents and later opinions applied it to newer steviol glycoside preparations.', '• Often blended with erythritol, other polyols or high-intensity sweeteners • Gut conversion to steviol is part of the metabolism • Repeated low-sugar products can stack exposure', '• Children with high low-calorie product intake • People tracking sweetener exposure • People sensitive to sugar alcohol blends used with stevia', 'Small amounts of sugar • Whole fruit sweetness • Unsweetened drinks • Herbs/spices for flavour', 'Approved. Use is subject to permitted food categories and maximum levels. Some newer steviol glycoside variants may differ between GB and Northern Ireland authorisation.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Stevia can sound more natural, but it is still a powerful sweetener. The Unboxed question is whether it is replacing sugar or keeping the sweet taste loop switched on.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2023.8387', '2026-07-05', 'ADI 4 mg/kg bw/day expressed as steviol equivalents. Note GB/NI differences for newer steviol glycoside categories where relevant.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0103', 'E960b', 'Steviol glycosides from fermentation', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Low-sugar drinks • Tabletop sweeteners • Yoghurts and desserts • Reduced-sugar sauces', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '4 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as steviol equivalents', 'Steviol glycosides are high-intensity sweeteners. EFSA assesses the steviol glycoside group using an ADI expressed as steviol equivalents.', '• Watch total sweetener pattern across drinks, yoghurts and diet foods • Often used with other sweeteners to improve taste • Does not remove the need to check the full ultra-processed product', '• People with sensitive digestion • Children consuming many low-sugar products • People trying to reduce sweet-taste dependence', 'Less sweetened recipes • Fruit-based flavouring • Reducing sweetness gradually', 'Approved for permitted uses and limits. Newer steviol glycoside subtypes have specific authorisation/specification rules.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a low-calorie sweetener, not a free pass. The bigger question is how often low-sugar products are replacing real food.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2024.9045', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0104', 'E960c', 'Enzymatically produced steviol glycosides', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Reduced-sugar drinks • Tabletop sweeteners • Low-sugar desserts • Dairy alternatives where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '4 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as steviol equivalents', 'Enzymatically produced steviol glycosides are assessed within the steviol glycoside safety framework. UK authorisation details have been updated for E960c.', '• Often blended with other sweeteners • Check repeated intake from diet drinks and desserts • Product limits and exact steviol glycoside type matter', '• People with sensitive digestion • Children with frequent low-sugar product intake • People reducing reliance on sweetened foods', 'Less sweetened recipes • Fruit-based flavouring • Gradual sweetness reduction', 'Approved for specific permitted uses and limits. GB authorisation for E960c has specific updated authorisation details.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; FSA regulated products register', 'Useful for reducing sugar, but still part of the sweetener pattern. Unboxed looks at the whole day, not just calories.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://data.food.gov.uk/regulated-products/food_authorisations/e-960c', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0105', 'E961', 'Neotame', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Low-sugar drinks • Desserts • Chewing gum • Tabletop sweeteners where permitted', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '2 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Neotame is an intense sweetener structurally related to aspartame but used at very low levels because it is much sweeter than sugar. EFSA established an ADI.', '• Often appears in low-calorie sweetener blends • Sweetener stacking can come from drinks, gum and desserts • Check labels if sensitive to intense sweeteners', '• People sensitive to intense sweeteners • Children consuming multiple diet products • People trying to reduce sweet-taste exposure', 'Less sweetened foods • Fruit-based flavouring • Reducing sweetness gradually', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Neotame is used in tiny amounts, but it belongs in the sweetener awareness group because exposure can stack across “diet” products.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0106', 'E962', 'Salt of aspartame-acesulfame', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener blend', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Diet drinks • Sugar-free gum • Low-calorie desserts • Tabletop sweeteners where permitted', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver • Kidneys', 'Usually within 24 hrs*', 'ADI follows components: aspartame 40 mg/kg bw/day; acesulfame K 9 mg/kg bw/day', 'This additive combines aspartame and acesulfame. Aspartame-containing foods require a phenylalanine warning for people with PKU.', '• Combines two sweeteners in one additive • Check for additional sweeteners in the same product • Caffeine in diet drinks can confuse perceived effects', '• People with PKU must avoid phenylalanine sources • People sensitive to diet drinks • Children with frequent diet product intake', 'Less sweetened drinks • Water/fruit infusions • Gradual sweetness reduction', 'Approved for permitted uses. Foods containing aspartame/aspartame-acesulfame must carry phenylalanine labelling.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'This one matters because it is not just “one sweetener”. It brings the aspartame/PKU warning and the wider diet-drink stacking issue.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0107', 'E964', 'Polyglycitol syrup', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener / humectant', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Chewing gum • Low-sugar chocolate • Protein bars and diet foods', 'Hours', 'Gut fermentation • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Polyglycitol syrup is a polyol-type bulk sweetener. Polyols can cause wind, bloating or laxative effects at higher intakes.', '• Polyols stack quickly in sugar-free sweets and bars • Products with >10% added polyols must warn about laxative effects • Gut response is dose-dependent', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People consuming protein/diet bars regularly', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened recipes • Whole-food snacks', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol laxative warning applies where added polyols exceed labelling thresholds.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'This is less about toxicity and more about the gut. Sugar-free does not always mean consequence-free.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0108', 'E965 (i)', 'Maltitol', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Low-sugar chocolate • Protein bars • Diabetic/diet products', 'Hours', 'Gut fermentation • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Maltitol is a polyol sweetener. It contributes fewer calories than sugar but can cause gas, bloating and laxative effects at higher intake.', '• Stacks with other polyols such as sorbitol, xylitol and erythritol • Common in bars and sugar-free sweets • Portion size strongly affects tolerance', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People using low-carb/protein bars daily', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened foods • Whole-food snacks', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol laxative warning applies where added polyols exceed labelling thresholds.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Maltitol is one of the big gut-effect sweeteners. The label may say sugar-free, but your gut still has to process it.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0109', 'E965 (ii)', 'Maltitol syrup', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener / humectant', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free confectionery • Low-sugar chocolate • Protein bars • Bakery fillings', 'Hours', 'Gut fermentation • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Maltitol syrup is used for sweetness, texture and moisture. Like other polyols, high intakes can produce gastrointestinal symptoms.', '• Polyol stacking from bars, sweets and low-sugar desserts • Portion size matters • Laxative warning applies above labelling thresholds', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People eating multiple diet products per day', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened recipes • Whole foods', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol laxative warning applies where added polyols exceed labelling thresholds.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'This is maltitol in syrup form. The practical check is the same: how many polyols are you eating in one day?', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0110', 'E966', 'Lactitol', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free sweets • Low-sugar chocolate • Chewing gum • Diet products', 'Hours', 'Gut fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Lactitol is a polyol sweetener. It can have laxative effects at higher intakes and may cause bloating or wind in sensitive individuals.', '• Stacks with other polyols • Larger portions increase gut effects • Check sugar-free sweets and “diabetic” products', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • People with lactose-related sensitivity may want to check tolerance', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened foods • Whole-food snacks', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol laxative warning applies where added polyols exceed labelling thresholds.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Lactitol sits in the sugar-free gut-effect group. The main issue is tolerance and dose, not fear.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0111', 'E967', 'Xylitol', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sugar-free gum • Mints • Tooth-friendly sweets • Low-sugar foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Xylitol is a polyol sweetener associated with reduced tooth demineralisation when replacing sugar, but high intakes can cause gut symptoms.', '• Polyol stacking can trigger bloating/laxative effects • Common in gum and mints across the day • Extremely toxic to dogs, so keep products away from pets', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • Children eating sugar-free sweets • Pet owners should keep xylitol away from dogs', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened foods • Non-sweet snacks', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol laxative warning applies where added polyols exceed labelling thresholds.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Xylitol can be useful for replacing sugar in gum, but dose still matters. Also: keep it away from dogs.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.nhs.uk/live-well/eat-well/food-types/are-sweeteners-safe/', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0112', 'E968', 'Erythritol', 'Sweeteners', 'Bulk sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Keto foods • Low-sugar chocolate • Protein bars • Tabletop sweeteners • Sugar-free drinks', 'Hours', 'Absorbed • Kidneys', 'Mostly within 24 hrs*', '0.5 g/kg body weight/day', 'EFSA re-evaluated erythritol and established an ADI based mainly on gastrointestinal tolerance. Large intakes may cause digestive symptoms.', '• Stacks with other polyols • Often used heavily in keto/low-sugar products • Portion size can be high compared with intense sweeteners', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • People eating keto/sugar-free products daily • Children consuming sugar-free sweets/drinks', 'Smaller portions • Less sweetened recipes • Whole-food snacks', 'Approved for permitted uses. Polyol labelling rules apply where thresholds are met.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Erythritol is absorbed differently from many polyols, but it still belongs in the “watch your total sugar-free intake” group.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ Strong', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2023.8430', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0113', 'E969', 'Advantame', 'Sweeteners', 'High-intensity sweetener', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Low-sugar drinks • Desserts • Chewing gum • Tabletop sweeteners where permitted', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', 'Usually within 24–48 hrs*', '5 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Advantame is a very high-intensity sweetener used at extremely low levels. EFSA established an ADI.', '• Often blended with other sweeteners • Sweetener exposure can stack across diet products • Check if you react to highly sweetened foods', '• People sensitive to intense sweeteners • Children consuming many diet products • People trying to reduce sweet-taste dependence', 'Less sweetened recipes • Water/fruit infusions • Gradual sweetness reduction', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Advantame is used in tiny amounts, but it still supports the same question: how much sweetener exposure is in your day?', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/sweeteners', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0114', 'E322', 'Lecithins', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / texture support', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Chocolate • Spreads • Bakery products • Margarine • Sauces', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Lecithins are emulsifiers that help mix fats and water. They are commonly derived from soy, sunflower or egg sources.', '• Check allergen/source labelling where relevant • Often appears in ultra-processed chocolate/spreads • Look at the whole emulsifier and fat system', '• People with soy or egg allergy depending on source • People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many chocolate/spread products', 'Egg yolk lecithin • Sunflower lecithin • Simpler recipes with fewer emulsifiers', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels. Allergen labelling applies where relevant.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Lecithin is usually lower concern, but it is a useful signpost for processed texture engineering.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0115', 'E400', 'Alginic acid', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Desserts • Ice cream • Sauces • Dairy alternatives • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Alginic acid is a seaweed-derived thickener. It is generally considered low concern at authorised food additive levels.', '• Can stack with other gums and thickeners • Large amounts of gums/fibres may affect bloating • Texture-engineered foods often contain several stabilisers', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake • Children consuming many processed desserts', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Chia/flax texture • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a seaweed-derived texture additive. For most people it is low awareness, but gum stacking can matter for digestion.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0116', 'E401', 'Sodium alginate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Ice cream • Sauces • Desserts • Dairy alternatives • Reformed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Sodium alginate is an alginate thickener/stabiliser. It helps foods hold texture and is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Often appears with other gums/stabilisers • Large texture stacks may cause bloating in sensitive people • Check processed desserts and sauces', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler thickening methods', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'A texture additive rather than a headline concern. The useful check is whether several thickeners are being stacked together.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0117', 'E402', 'Potassium alginate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Desserts • Sauces • Ice cream • Dairy alternatives • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Potassium alginate is a seaweed-derived stabiliser. It is used for texture and is generally low concern at approved use levels.', '• Check with other gums and stabilisers • Larger gum loads may affect gut comfort • Common in texture-engineered products', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking stabiliser intake', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Part of the alginate family. Usually low concern, but it tells you the product has been texture-engineered.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0118', 'E403', 'Ammonium alginate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Desserts • Sauces • Ice cream • Processed foods where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Ammonium alginate is part of the alginate group used for thickening and stabilising foods.', '• Watch total gum/stabiliser stack • May contribute to bloating in sensitive people when many gums are present • More relevant in highly processed texture systems', '• People with sensitive digestion • People reducing gum/thickener intake', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Not a major standalone concern. It is useful as a marker of how much texture engineering is in the product.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0119', 'E404', 'Calcium alginate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Reformed foods • Desserts • Sauces • Bakery fillings • Specialist food gels', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Calcium alginate is used to form gels and stabilise textures. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Can appear in reformed or structured foods • Check for multiple gums and stabilisers • Gut comfort depends on total texture-additive load', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking reformed/structured foods', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Calcium alginate helps build structure. It is less about danger and more about recognising engineered texture.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0120', 'E405', 'Propane-1,2-diol alginate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Salad dressings • Sauces • Beer foam stabilisation where permitted • Desserts • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '55 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Propane-1,2-diol alginate is an alginate derivative used to stabilise textures and emulsions. EFSA established a numerical ADI.', '• More chemically modified than simple alginates • Often used in sauces/dressings with emulsifier systems • Check processed sauce and drink patterns', '• People with sensitive digestion • People eating many sauces/dressings • Children with high processed sauce intake', 'Pectin • Mustard/egg-based emulsions where suitable • Simpler dressings', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This sits slightly above simple alginates for awareness because it has its own ADI and appears in processed texture systems.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0121', 'E406', 'Agar', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Gelling agent / thickener', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Desserts • Jellies • Vegetarian gel products • Bakery fillings • Specialist foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Agar is a seaweed-derived gelling agent. It is generally low concern at normal food additive levels.', '• Can add to total gum/fibre load • Large amounts may affect bowel habits • Often used in vegetarian jelly-style products', '• People with sensitive digestion • People adjusting fibre/gum intake', 'Gelatine where suitable • Pectin • Fruit-based gels', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Agar is usually one of the easier texture additives to understand. It gels food, and for most people it is low concern.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0122', 'E407', 'Carrageenan', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Dairy alternatives • Flavoured milk • Cream products • Desserts • Processed meats where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'Temporary group ADI 75 mg/kg body weight/day for E407/E407a', 'EFSA re-evaluated carrageenan and processed eucheuma seaweed together. Food-grade carrageenan is distinct from degraded carrageenan/poligeenan, which is not authorised as a food additive.', '• Watch with other gums and emulsifiers • Relevant in dairy alternatives and creamy processed foods • Infant/medical food uses have had specific EFSA follow-up questions', '• People with sensitive digestion or IBD concerns may choose to monitor tolerance • Parents checking infant/specialist foods • People tracking emulsifier/gum intake', 'Pectin • Gelatine where suitable • Agar • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels, with specifications. EFSA has requested follow-up data for some uses/technical issues.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Carrageenan is one to explain carefully. The key is food-grade use, gut tolerance, and how many thickeners are stacked in the same diet.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2018.5238', '2026-07-07', 'Public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance. Avoid presenting clearance as a guaranteed medical detox time; individual response varies by dose, diet, gut function and health status.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0123', 'E407a', 'Processed eucheuma seaweed', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Dairy alternatives • Desserts • Processed meat products • Sauces • Reformed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'Temporary group ADI 75 mg/kg body weight/day for E407/E407a', 'Processed eucheuma seaweed is a seaweed-derived gelling and thickening additive assessed together with carrageenan. Food-grade use is permitted, with attention to specifications.', '• Watch with carrageenan and other gums • Gum/emulsifier stacks may affect gut comfort in sensitive people • More relevant in texture-engineered foods', '• People with sensitive digestion • People with IBD concerns who track triggers • Children consuming many processed desserts or dairy alternatives', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels, with specifications.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This sits in the carrageenan family. It is not about panic; it is about recognising repeated gum and texture stacks.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2018.5238', '2026-07-07', 'EFSA 2018 re-evaluation set temporary group ADI for carrageenan and processed eucheuma seaweed; specifications remain important.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0124', 'E410', 'Locust bean gum; carob gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Ice cream • Dairy alternatives • Sauces • Desserts • Gluten-free foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Locust bean gum is a plant seed gum used to thicken and stabilise foods. EFSA reported no safety concern at authorised uses.', '• Can stack with guar gum, xanthan gum, carrageenan or alginates • Larger gum loads may affect bloating • Common in texture-engineered products', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake • Children consuming many processed desserts', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'A lower-awareness gum, but it still tells you a product has been built for texture and shelf stability.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Seed gum; public-facing timeline is practical Unboxed guidance, not a guaranteed clearance claim.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0125', 'E412', 'Guar gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Sauces • Ice cream • Dairy alternatives • Gluten-free products • Protein products', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Guar gum is a plant fibre/gum used to thicken foods. It is generally low concern at authorised food additive levels, but can affect gut comfort in some people.', '• Stacks with xanthan gum, carrageenan and other fibres • Can increase total fermentable fibre load • Common in protein bars and gluten-free products', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • People increasing fibre rapidly • Children consuming many gum-containing products', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler thickening methods', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Guar gum is not usually the headline concern. The useful question is how many gums and fibres are stacked in one day.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Gut effects relate mainly to dose and tolerance; avoid presenting normal food additive use as harmful.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0126', 'E413', 'Tragacanth', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Confectionery • Sauces • Dressings • Bakery decorations • Specialist foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Tragacanth is a plant gum used as a thickener and stabiliser. Available evaluations do not indicate a general safety concern at authorised levels.', '• Look out for total gum load • May appear with colours and acids in sweets • Gut tolerance varies by person', '• People with sensitive digestion • People with rare gum sensitivities • Children eating many confectionery products', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Tragacanth is a texture additive. Most people do not need to focus on it alone, but it can add to the overall gum stack.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging/Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Older gum with less consumer-facing evidence than common gums; keep claims conservative.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0127', 'E414', 'Acacia gum; gum arabic', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Soft drinks • Sweets • Flavour emulsions • Chewing gum • Glazes/coatings', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Acacia gum is a plant exudate gum used for stabilising and carrying flavours. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Can add to total fibre/gum intake • Often used in flavour emulsions and coatings • May appear alongside colours, acids and sweeteners', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/fibre intake • People with rare acacia sensitivity', 'Pectin • Simpler flavour systems • Fruit-based ingredients', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Gum arabic is usually low awareness. In Unboxed terms, it is more of a stacking marker than a standalone concern.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Common stabiliser/carrier; gut tolerance depends on total intake.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0128', 'E415', 'Xanthan gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Sauces • Dressings • Gluten-free bread • Dairy alternatives • Protein products', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Xanthan gum is a fermentation-derived gum widely used to thicken and stabilise foods. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Frequently stacked with guar gum, locust bean gum and carrageenan • Can contribute to bloating in sensitive people • Common in gluten-free and low-fat texture systems', '• People with IBS or sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake • Children consuming many processed sauces/desserts', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Xanthan gum is common because it works. The Unboxed check is whether it appears as part of a bigger texture stack.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Fermentation-derived gum; public-facing issue is mainly tolerance/stacking, not toxicity.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0129', 'E416', 'Karaya gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Confectionery • Sauces • Dressings • Specialist diet foods • Glazes', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Karaya gum is a plant gum used as a thickener and stabiliser. It is generally considered low concern at authorised levels.', '• Adds to total gum load • May appear with colours, acids and sweeteners in confectionery • Digestive tolerance varies', '• People with sensitive digestion • People with gum sensitivities • Children with high confectionery intake', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Karaya gum is not usually a headline additive. It matters most when a label contains several gums or texture agents.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging/Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Keep claims conservative; uncommon gum with limited public evidence compared with xanthan/guar.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0130', 'E417', 'Tara gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Ice cream • Dairy alternatives • Sauces • Desserts • Bakery fillings', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Tara gum is a seed gum used to thicken and stabilise foods. EFSA has considered it low concern at authorised levels.', '• Often used with other gums • Gum stacking may affect gut comfort • Common in creamy or gelled products', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another lower-awareness texture gum. It is useful mainly as a signal to check the whole stabiliser system.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Seed gum; no specific warning for general population at permitted levels.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0131', 'E418', 'Gellan gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Dairy alternatives • Drinks with suspended ingredients • Desserts • Jellies • Sauces', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Gellan gum is a fermentation-derived gelling agent. It helps suspend particles and form gels, and is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Often used in plant milks and drinks • Can stack with carrageenan, guar, xanthan or alginates • Larger gum load may affect gut comfort', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking dairy alternative ingredients • Children consuming many gelled drinks/desserts', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Gellan gum is common in modern plant-based products. The bigger question is the full texture system, not one gum.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Fermentation-derived gum; common in plant milks.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0132', 'E425', 'Konjac', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / gelling agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Konjac noodles • Jelly desserts where permitted • Low-calorie foods • Thickened products • Diet foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Konjac gum/glucomannan is a high-viscosity fibre used for thickening and gelling. The main practical concerns are digestive tolerance and safe product form.', '• Can swell and thicken strongly • Stacks with other fibres/gums • Product form matters, especially gelled sweets or choking-prone formats', '• Children • People with swallowing difficulties • People with IBS or sensitive digestion • People using konjac/glucomannan supplements', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Smaller portions of fibre-rich ingredients', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels, with product-specific restrictions where relevant.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; NHS', 'Konjac is not just a normal gum. Its strong gelling and swelling behaviour means form and portion matter.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Konjac/gelling product restrictions should be handled carefully; avoid blanket claims.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0133', 'E426', 'Soybean hemicellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Sauces • Bakery products • Drinks • Dairy alternatives • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Soybean hemicellulose is a soy-derived fibre additive used to stabilise and improve texture. General safety concern is low at authorised levels.', '• Source is soy-derived, so allergen/source awareness matters • Can stack with other fibres and gums • Common in texture-engineered foods', '• People with soy allergy or sensitivity • People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/fibre intake', 'Pectin • Sunflower-derived alternatives where available • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels. Allergen labelling applies where relevant.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'The main thing here is source awareness. If soy matters to you, this is one to spot on the label.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Soy-derived additive; keep allergen wording factual and do not assume all soy-allergic people react to refined fractions.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0134', 'E427', 'Cassia gum', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / gelling agent', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Ice cream • Dairy desserts • Sauces • Pet-food style gel systems not relevant to food labels • Specialist foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Cassia gum is a seed gum used for thickening and gelling. EFSA evaluations support authorised uses within permitted specifications.', '• Often used with carrageenan or other gums • Specifications matter for impurity control • Gum stacking may affect gut comfort', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake', 'Pectin • Agar • Gelatine where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Cassia gum is another texture builder. The useful check is whether several gums are doing the job of real food structure.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Seed gum; specifications are important.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0135', 'E432', 'Polyoxyethylene sorbitan monolaurate; Polysorbate 20', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Sauces • Desserts • Ice cream • Flavour emulsions • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI for polysorbates E432–E436', 'Polysorbates are emulsifiers used to keep oil and water mixed. EFSA set a group ADI for polysorbates and considered authorised uses within this framework.', '• Belongs to polysorbate group E432–E436 • Watch with other emulsifiers such as E471 and E466 • Emulsifier stacking is relevant to gut-focused users', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed desserts/sauces', 'Lecithin • Egg-based emulsions where suitable • Mustard • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Polysorbates are worth higher awareness because they are true emulsifiers and often appear in ultra-processed textures.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Group ADI commonly applied to polysorbates E432-E436; confirm specific EFSA opinion before public launch.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0136', 'E433', 'Polyoxyethylene sorbitan mono-oleate; Polysorbate 80', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Ice cream • Desserts • Sauces • Dressings • Processed bakery fillings', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI for polysorbates E432–E436', 'Polysorbate 80 is an emulsifier used to stabilise mixtures of fat and water. It is permitted within authorised levels and group safety assessments.', '• Watch alongside E466 carboxymethyl cellulose in some processed foods • Emulsifier stacks may matter for gut tolerance • Often appears in creamy processed foods', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking emulsifier exposure • Children consuming many processed desserts', 'Lecithin • Egg-based emulsions where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Polysorbate 80 is one of the emulsifiers people should recognise. The issue is the wider emulsifier pattern across the day.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Emulsifier. Include research nuance; animal/in-vitro findings should not be overstated as proven human harm.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0137', 'E434', 'Polyoxyethylene sorbitan monopalmitate; Polysorbate 40', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Desserts • Sauces • Flavour emulsions • Bakery fillings • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI for polysorbates E432–E436', 'Polysorbate 40 is part of the polysorbate emulsifier group. It supports stable mixtures and textures in processed foods.', '• Additive exposure should be considered across the polysorbate group • Watch with other emulsifiers and gums • More relevant in ultra-processed foods', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People with sensitive digestion • Children with high processed dessert intake', 'Lecithin • Mustard/egg-based emulsions where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This belongs in the emulsifier awareness group. It is less about one product and more about how often emulsifier-built foods show up.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Polysorbate group entry; use conservative wording.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0138', 'E435', 'Polyoxyethylene sorbitan monostearate; Polysorbate 60', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Cakes • Desserts • Ice cream • Sauces • Processed bakery products', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI for polysorbates E432–E436', 'Polysorbate 60 is an emulsifier used to maintain stable textures in processed foods, especially fat/water systems.', '• Part of polysorbate group exposure • Watch with E471, E475 and other emulsifiers • Common in soft, long-life textures', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People with sensitive digestion • Families reducing ultra-processed bakery foods', 'Lecithin • Egg-based emulsions where suitable • Simpler baking recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Polysorbate 60 is a useful marker of engineered texture, especially in cakes and desserts that need to stay soft.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Polysorbate group entry; confirm individual food categories before app launch.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0139', 'E436', 'Polyoxyethylene sorbitan tristearate; Polysorbate 65', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Desserts • Bakery products • Flavour emulsions • Processed foods where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI for polysorbates E432–E436', 'Polysorbate 65 is part of the polysorbate emulsifier group used to stabilise processed food textures.', '• Consider total polysorbate group intake • Watch with other emulsifiers and stabilisers • More likely in ultra-processed formulations than simple foods', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People with sensitive digestion • Children with high processed food intake', 'Lecithin • Simpler emulsions • Shorter ingredient lists', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another polysorbate. The Unboxed view is to watch the group pattern, not just memorise each number.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Polysorbate group entry; public detail should link to group assessment.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0140', 'E440', 'Pectins', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / gelling agent / stabiliser', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Jam • Fruit fillings • Yoghurts • Desserts • Low-sugar preserves', 'Hours', 'Gut • Fermentation', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Pectin is a plant fibre used for gelling and thickening, especially in fruit-based foods. It is generally low concern at food additive levels.', '• Can add to total fibre/gum load • Often appears with sugar or sweeteners in jams/desserts • Gut tolerance depends on portion', '• People with sensitive digestion • People adjusting fibre intake • Children consuming many sweet preserves/desserts', 'Fruit pectin • Chia/flax texture • Simpler fruit reductions', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Pectin is one of the more familiar texture additives. It is usually low concern, but still part of the wider texture stack.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Pectins include amidated pectin where applicable; keep as group unless separate row exists.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0141', 'E442', 'Ammonium phosphatides', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Chocolate • Cocoa products • Fat-based confectionery • Coatings • Spreads where permitted', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '30 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Ammonium phosphatides are emulsifiers used mainly in chocolate and fat-based products. EFSA established an ADI.', '• Mainly a chocolate/fat-based product additive • Watch with lecithin, PGPR and other chocolate emulsifiers • Also consider sugar and fat load', '• Children eating chocolate frequently • People tracking emulsifier intake • People reducing ultra-processed confectionery', 'Lecithin • Better conching/process control • Simpler chocolate formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a chocolate-texture additive. It matters less on its own than the overall confectionery pattern.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'ADI 30 mg/kg bw/day commonly reported for ammonium phosphatides; verify specific EFSA URL before public launch.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0142', 'E444', 'Sucrose acetate isobutyrate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Stabiliser / weighting agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Flavoured soft drinks • Citrus drinks • Cloudy drinks where permitted • Beverage emulsions', 'Hours', 'Liver • Digestion', '24–48 hrs varies*', '10 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Sucrose acetate isobutyrate is used to stabilise flavour oils in drinks. EFSA/JECFA set an ADI for its authorised uses.', '• Mainly relevant in soft drinks • Look for use with colours, acids, preservatives and sweeteners • Daily drink habits matter', '• Children consuming many soft drinks • People tracking additive load in drinks • People reducing ultra-processed beverages', 'Whole fruit infusions • Simpler drinks • No-oil flavour systems', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is one to know if you drink flavoured soft drinks often. It helps keep flavour oils suspended in the drink.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Beverage stabiliser; ADI 10 mg/kg bw/day commonly used.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0143', 'E445', 'Glycerol esters of wood rosins', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Stabiliser / weighting agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Citrus-flavoured soft drinks • Cloudy drinks • Beverage emulsions • Flavoured drinks where permitted', 'Hours', 'Liver • Digestion', '24–48 hrs varies*', '12.5 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Glycerol esters of wood rosins help stabilise flavour oils in drinks. EFSA/JECFA established safety guidance for authorised uses.', '• Mainly a soft drink additive • Often appears with acids, colours, preservatives or sweeteners • Repeated beverage exposure matters', '• Children consuming many soft drinks • People tracking drink additive stacks • People with resin/rosin sensitivity concerns', 'Simpler drinks • Fruit infusions • Natural flavour systems without weighting agents', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is a drink-stabiliser additive. The Unboxed check is not one can, but how often these drink systems appear.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Beverage weighting agent; ADI 12.5 mg/kg bw/day commonly referenced.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0144', 'E460', 'Cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Bulking agent / stabiliser / anti-caking agent', '🟢 Low Awareness', '1/5', '• Grated cheese • Low-calorie foods • Bakery products • Sauces • Powdered mixes', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Cellulose is a plant fibre used for bulk, texture and anti-caking. It is generally low concern at authorised food additive levels.', '• Can add to fibre/bulking load • Often appears in reduced-calorie or anti-caking systems • Look at the whole processed product', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking fibre/bulking agents • Children consuming many processed snacks', 'Whole-food fibre • Simpler recipes • Fresh grated cheese instead of anti-caking blends', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Cellulose is basically a fibre function in food processing. Usually low concern, but it can show how engineered a product is.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Includes microcrystalline/powdered cellulose depending label; check if split needed later.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0145', 'E461', 'Methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Meat alternatives • Gluten-free products • Sauces • Bakery products • Desserts', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Methyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used to bind, thicken and stabilise foods. It is generally low concern at permitted levels.', '• Often used in meat alternatives for binding/texture • Can stack with other cellulose gums • Large gum/fibre load may affect digestion', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking meat-alternative additives • People reducing modified cellulose gums', 'Egg binding where suitable • Psyllium/chia/flax in recipes • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a texture-builder, especially in meat alternatives. It is a useful marker for how much structure is being engineered.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Modified cellulose. Avoid overstating gut effects; focus on tolerance and stacking.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0146', 'E462', 'Ethyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Stabiliser / coating agent / binder', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Encapsulated ingredients • Food coatings • Specialist processed foods • Flavour systems where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Ethyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used in coatings, encapsulation and stabilisation. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• More relevant in specialist formulations than everyday whole foods • Can appear in coating/encapsulation systems • Check with other modified celluloses', '• People tracking modified cellulose additives • People with sensitive digestion', 'Simpler coatings • Direct ingredients without encapsulation where feasible', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Ethyl cellulose is mostly about food technology: coatings, binding and controlled texture rather than a flavour or colour.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Modified cellulose; uses may be more specialised.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0147', 'E463', 'Hydroxypropyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Sauces • Desserts • Gluten-free foods • Bakery products • Specialist processed foods', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Hydroxypropyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used for thickening and stabilising. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Belongs to modified cellulose gum group • Watch with other gums and emulsifiers • May contribute to texture stack in processed foods', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking gum/thickener intake', 'Pectin • Psyllium/chia/flax in recipes • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another modified cellulose. Not usually a standalone worry, but it shows how texture is being built.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Modified cellulose additive; no numerical ADI required in many evaluations.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0148', 'E464', 'Hydroxypropyl methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Gluten-free bread • Meat alternatives • Sauces • Desserts • Bakery products', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Hydroxypropyl methyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used to improve texture, water binding and stability.', '• Common in gluten-free and plant-based texture systems • Can stack with methyl cellulose and other gums • Gut comfort depends on total additive/fibre load', '• People with sensitive digestion • People eating many gluten-free/plant-based processed foods • People tracking modified celluloses', 'Psyllium • Chia/flax • Egg where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is common in products that need structure without gluten or meat. It is a texture signal more than a headline danger.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'HPMC modified cellulose; practical relevance in gluten-free/plant-based foods.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0149', 'E465', 'Ethyl methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Sauces • Desserts • Bakery products • Specialist processed foods • Texture systems', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Ethyl methyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used for stabilising and texture control. It is generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Watch modified cellulose stacking • More relevant in processed texture systems • Total gum/fibre load may affect gut comfort', '• People with sensitive digestion • People tracking modified cellulose additives', 'Pectin • Simpler recipes • Psyllium/chia/flax where suitable', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Another texture-support additive. The bigger story is the number of gums and stabilisers in the final product.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Modified cellulose; conservative public wording.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0150', 'E466', 'Carboxy methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / emulsifier', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Ice cream • Sauces • Dressings • Bakery products • Dairy alternatives', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Carboxymethyl cellulose is a cellulose gum used to thicken and stabilise foods. It has been discussed in gut/emulsifier research, but approved food uses remain regulated.', '• Watch with polysorbate 80 and other emulsifiers • Common in creamy processed foods • Gut-focused users may want to track repeated exposure', '• People with sensitive digestion • People with IBD concerns who monitor triggers • Children consuming many emulsified desserts/sauces', 'Pectin • Guar/locust bean gum where tolerated • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is one of the texture additives worth recognising. Not because one serving proves harm, but because it sits in the emulsifier/gut conversation.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Do not overstate emerging gut research. Present as higher awareness due to emulsifier discussions and repeated exposure relevance.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0151', 'E468', 'Crosslinked sodium carboxy methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Stabiliser / disintegrant / texture agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Specialist processed foods • Supplements/tablets where relevant • Texture systems • Foods where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Crosslinked sodium carboxymethyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used for texture and structure functions. It is less common as an everyday label additive.', '• Related to cellulose gum family • Watch with other modified celluloses • Mostly relevant in highly processed/specialist formulations', '• People tracking modified cellulose additives • People with sensitive digestion', 'Simpler formulations • Pectin or fibre-based texture where suitable', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a more technical cellulose additive. If it appears, it usually tells you the product is highly formulated.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging/Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Specialist modified cellulose. Keep claims limited.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0152', 'E469', 'Enzymatically hydrolysed carboxy methyl cellulose', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Thickener / stabiliser / texture agent', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Specialist processed foods • Sauces • Desserts • Texture systems where permitted', 'Hours', 'Gut • Minimal absorption', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Enzymatically hydrolysed carboxymethyl cellulose is a modified cellulose used for texture and stabilisation. It belongs to the cellulose gum family.', '• Related to E466 cellulose gum • Watch with other emulsifiers and gums • Relevant mainly in highly formulated foods', '• People tracking cellulose gums • People with sensitive digestion', 'Pectin • Simpler recipes • Less processed texture systems', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a label to notice when you are tracking texture additives. It is part of the bigger cellulose gum picture.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging/Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Related to CMC; public summary should avoid implying identical evidence to E466 unless specifically supported.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0153', 'E470a', 'Sodium, potassium and calcium salts of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / anti-caking agent / stabiliser', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Powdered foods • Bakery products • Confectionery • Supplements/tablets • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'These are salts of fatty acids used for anti-caking, emulsifying and stabilising. They are generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Often used in powdered or processed formulations • Watch with other emulsifiers in long ingredient lists • Not the same issue as industrial trans fats', '• People tracking emulsifier/anti-caking agents • People with sensitive digestion', 'Simpler powder blends • Lecithin where suitable • Better processing/packaging', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a functional processing additive. Usually lower concern, but it is another sign of formulation complexity.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Fatty acid salts; typically low toxicological concern.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0154', 'E470b', 'Magnesium salts of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / anti-caking agent / stabiliser', '🟢 Low/Moderate Awareness', '2/5', '• Powdered foods • Confectionery • Bakery products • Supplements/tablets • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Magnesium salts of fatty acids are used as anti-caking agents, emulsifiers and stabilisers. They are generally low concern at authorised levels.', '• Often appears in powders/tablets or processed formulations • Can stack with other anti-caking agents • Check the whole product rather than this additive alone', '• People tracking anti-caking/emulsifier additives • People with sensitive digestion', 'Simpler powder blends • Better storage/packaging • Lecithin where suitable', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is usually a low-awareness additive. It tells you more about processing and powder flow than health risk.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Magnesium fatty acid salts; keep lower awareness unless product context suggests otherwise.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0155', 'E471', 'Mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Cakes • Margarine/spreads • Ice cream • Processed bakery foods', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'Mono- and diglycerides are common emulsifiers used to improve texture, softness and stability. They are widely used in bakery and fat-based foods.', '• Common ultra-processed bread/cake marker • Often used with E472 esters and other emulsifiers • Look at daily bakery exposure', '• People reducing ultra-processed bread • People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating packaged bakery foods daily', 'Egg/lecithin where suitable • Sourdough/shorter shelf-life bread • Traditional baking methods', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'E471 is one of the big label markers for modern soft processed bread and cakes. It is about texture, shelf life and repeated exposure.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Very common emulsifier. Origin can be plant/animal depending supplier; vegetarian status requires manufacturer info.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0156', 'E472a', 'Acetic acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Cakes • Whipped toppings • Margarine/spreads • Processed bakery foods', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472a is an emulsifier made from mono- and diglycerides esterified with acetic acid. It supports texture and stability in processed foods.', '• Often used with E471 and other E472 emulsifiers • Bread/cake texture stack matters • Check processed bakery foods eaten daily', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating packaged bakery foods often • People reducing ultra-processed foods', 'Traditional baking methods • Lecithin where suitable • Shorter shelf-life recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This belongs in the processed bakery emulsifier group. One ingredient may look small, but the daily stack can be big.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'E472 group entries should remain consistent; avoid unsupported claims about origin.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0157', 'E472b', 'Lactic acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bread • Cakes • Desserts • Whipped products • Margarine/spreads', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472b is an emulsifier used to improve texture, aeration and stability in processed foods.', '• Often used with E471/E472 emulsifier blends • Relevant in soft bakery and whipped textures • Watch repeated exposure from packaged bakery foods', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed bakery products • People reducing ultra-processed foods', 'Traditional baking • Egg/lecithin where suitable • Shorter ingredient lists', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is another bakery/texture additive. The Unboxed check is how often foods need emulsifiers to feel fresh.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Lactic acid ester of mono/diglycerides.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0158', 'E472c', 'Citric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser / antioxidant support', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Margarine/spreads • Bakery products • Desserts • Processed fats • Sauces', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472c is an emulsifier used in fat-containing foods to improve stability and texture.', '• Often appears in fat-based processed foods • Watch with E471 and other emulsifiers • The underlying fat/oil quality also matters', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People reducing processed fats • Children eating many processed desserts/bakery foods', 'Lecithin • Simpler fat blends • Traditional recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is a texture and fat-stability additive. It is more useful as a processing marker than a standalone red flag.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Citric acid ester; group safety generally low concern at permitted levels.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0159', 'E472d', 'Tartaric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Desserts • Processed fats • Whipped products where permitted', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472d is an emulsifier used to support texture and stability in processed foods.', '• Part of the E472 emulsifier family • Check alongside E471 and flour improvers • Mostly relevant in processed bakery/texture systems', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People reducing ultra-processed bakery foods', 'Traditional baking • Lecithin where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This belongs to the same bakery texture family. If you see several E47x additives together, the product is being engineered for texture.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'E472 group entry; no special standalone warning identified for general population.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0160', 'E472e', 'Mono- and diacetyltartaric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / flour treatment support', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Industrial bread • Rolls • Pizza bases • Bakery products • Frozen dough products', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472e, often known as DATEM, is widely used in industrial baking to improve dough strength, volume and texture.', '• Strong marker of industrial bread/dough systems • Often appears with E471, enzymes and flour treatment agents • Daily bread exposure can make this more relevant', '• People reducing ultra-processed bread • Children eating packaged bread daily • People tracking emulsifier/flour improver intake', 'Sourdough • Traditional long-fermentation bread • Shorter shelf-life bakery products', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'DATEM is one of the big modern bread markers. It shows how far bread can move from flour, water, salt and time.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'DATEM. Higher awareness due to common industrial bread relevance, not because approved uses are deemed unsafe.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0161', 'E472f', 'Mixed acetic and tartaric acid esters of mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Dough systems • Desserts • Processed fats where permitted', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / not allocated', 'E472f is an emulsifier used to support texture and stability, especially in processed bakery and fat systems.', '• Part of the E471/E472 emulsifier stack • Watch processed bakery frequency • Often used where texture and shelf-life are engineered', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • People reducing ultra-processed bakery foods • Children eating many packaged baked goods', 'Traditional baking • Sourdough • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is another E47x texture additive. The pattern matters more than memorising every member of the group.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'E472 group entry.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0162', 'E473', 'Sucrose esters of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Desserts • Drinks • Sauces • Fat-based products', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '40 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI with sucroglycerides where applicable', 'Sucrose esters of fatty acids are emulsifiers used to stabilise textures and mixtures. EFSA has assessed them with a numerical ADI.', '• Watch with other emulsifiers and gums • May appear in low-fat or highly stable texture systems • Exposure comes from multiple processed foods, not one product alone', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed bakery/dessert products • People with sensitive digestion', 'Lecithin • Egg/mustard emulsions where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Sucrose esters are functional emulsifiers. They are worth knowing because they help create modern processed textures.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'ADI 40 mg/kg bw/day commonly used for sucrose esters/sucroglycerides group; verify exact grouping at publication.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0163', 'E474', 'Sucroglycerides', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Desserts • Drinks • Sauces • Fat-based products', 'Hours', 'Digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '40 mg/kg body weight/day group ADI with E473 where applicable', 'Sucroglycerides are emulsifiers made from sucrose and edible fats. EFSA assesses them with sucrose esters because exposure from the group can be relevant.', '• Group exposure with E473 matters • Often appears with other emulsifiers and gums • Watch repeated intake from low-fat or highly stabilised foods', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed desserts/bakery products • People with sensitive digestion', 'Lecithin • Egg/mustard emulsions where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'Sucroglycerides are part of the texture-building system in processed foods. The pattern to watch is repeated emulsifier exposure, not one product alone.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://efsa.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.2903/j.efsa.2023.7961', '2026-07-07', 'Timeline fields are practical Unboxed guidance, not a medical clearance promise. Use additive-specific toxicokinetic evidence where available.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0164', 'E475', 'Polyglycerol esters of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Desserts • Margarine/spreads • Sauces • Whipped products', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day', 'Polyglycerol esters are emulsifiers used to stabilise fats, improve texture and maintain structure. EFSA has re-evaluated this group and set an ADI.', '• Often used with other emulsifiers • Common in fat-based processed foods • Texture stacks matter across the day', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed desserts/bakery foods • People reducing ultra-processed fats', 'Lecithin • Egg-based emulsions where suitable • Simpler recipes', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is another emulsifier that helps processed foods hold together. Look for how often emulsifier-built foods appear in the day.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Timeline fields are practical Unboxed guidance, not a medical clearance promise. Use additive-specific toxicokinetic evidence where available.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0165', 'E476', 'Polyglycerol polyricinoleate', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / viscosity reducer', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Chocolate • Cocoa products • Low-fat spreads • Confectionery fillings', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day', 'PGPR is used to reduce viscosity, especially in chocolate and cocoa products. EFSA revised the ADI upward after re-evaluation.', '• Common in chocolate/confectionery texture systems • Watch with high sugar and fat intake • Often signals ultra-processed confectionery', '• Children eating confectionery regularly • People tracking emulsifier intake • People reducing ultra-processed snacks', 'Cocoa butter • Lecithin • Traditional chocolate formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'PGPR is one of those additives that explains why cheap chocolate flows and sets differently. The bigger issue is the confectionery habit around it.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/4743', '2026-07-07', 'Timeline fields are practical Unboxed guidance, not a medical clearance promise. Use additive-specific toxicokinetic evidence where available.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0166', 'E477', 'Propane-1,2-diol esters of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / stabiliser', '🟡 Moderate Awareness', '3/5', '• Bakery products • Desserts • Whipped toppings • Fat spreads • Processed foods', 'Hours', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', '25 mg/kg body weight/day expressed as propane-1,2-diol', 'E477 is hydrolysed partly to propane-1,2-diol and fatty acids. EFSA re-evaluated it and retained an ADI expressed as propane-1,2-diol.', '• Check with other emulsifiers • Often appears in highly structured fat/foam foods • Repeated processed dessert intake matters', '• People tracking emulsifier intake • Children eating many processed desserts • People avoiding propylene glycol-related additive groups', 'Lecithin • Egg/cream-based recipes • Simpler formulations', 'Approved for permitted uses and levels.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA; JECFA', 'This is a functional texture additive. It is worth knowing because it sits in the same processed dessert and bakery pattern as many emulsifiers.', '⭐⭐⭐⭐ Moderate', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/efsajournal/pub/5497', '2026-07-07', 'Timeline fields are practical Unboxed guidance, not a medical clearance promise. Use additive-specific toxicokinetic evidence where available.', TRUE),
  ('UAL-0167', 'E479b', 'Thermally oxidised soya bean oil interacted with mono- and diglycerides of fatty acids', 'Emulsifiers, stabilisers, thickeners and gelling agents', 'Emulsifier / anti-spattering agent', '🟠 Higher Awareness', '4/5', '• Frying oils where permitted • Specialist fat preparations • Industrial frying applications', 'Hours to 1 day', 'Fat digestion • Liver', '24–48 hrs varies*', 'ADI not specified / limited permitted uses', 'This additive is used in specialist frying fat applications. Awareness is higher because it is a chemically modified oil/fat additive with narrow uses.', '• Relevant mainly in frying systems • Check overall fried-food and refined-oil exposure • Heat and repeated frying are the bigger context', '• People reducing fried/industrial foods • People tracking processed oil additives • People with soya allergy should check wider label/allergen controls', 'Better frying oil management • Fresh oils • Oven/air-fried alternatives where appropriate', 'Approved for specific permitted uses and limits.', 'FSA/GOV.UK; EFSA', 'This is not an everyday label for most people, but it belongs in the processed oil/frying awareness group. The food context matters most.', '⭐⭐⭐ Emerging / limited public detail', 'Batch detail checked', 'https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/approved-additives-and-e-numbers/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.food.gov.uk/business-guidance/approved-additives-and-e-numbers', 'https://www.efsa.europa.eu/en/topics/topic/food-additives', '2026-07-07', 'Timeline fields are practical Unboxed guidance, not a medical clearance promise. Use additive-specific toxicokinetic evidence where available.', TRUE),
